\section{Piklab}

Beginning with Piklab, I first validated the development environment using the provided \texttt{led\_blinker.asm} code. My prior experience with microprocessor programming from the "Computers and Microprocessors" course enabled rapid verification of this basic functionality. 

To systematically verify the microprocessor implementation, I adapted my original decoder testbench approach to assembly language. The testbench evaluated instruction groups sequentially, with hardware resets between test segments. Since assembly lacks direct hardware reset capability, I implemented this testing framework through three separate projects:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{testbench1.piklab} (First instruction group)
    \item \texttt{testbench2.piklab} (Second instruction group)
    \item \texttt{testbench3.piklab} (Third instruction group)
\end{itemize}

During initial testing, development was hindered by compilation failures in the provided \texttt{hex\_file\_reader} utility. This toolchain limitation prevented both successful builds and debugging of the assembly testbenches, requiring alternative verification methods.


\section{Final Testbench Implementation}

After resolving the toolchain issues, I successfully implemented the testbench using the corrected \texttt{hex\_file\_reader} utility. The testbench instantiates \texttt{pic\_rtl} along with all associated signals.

\subsection{Program Memory Loading Mechanism}
The instruction loading process utilizes the \texttt{hex\_file\_reader} package, specifically the \texttt{read\_ihex\_file} procedure. This procedure requires:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Input: The file path of assembly code generated by Piklab (via "Build Project")
    \item Output: An array containing the program memory (instructions in hexadecimal format)
\end{itemize}

Initial implementation attempts directly connected a \texttt{program\_memory} signal as the procedure output. However, compilation failed because:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The procedure requires a \texttt{variable} type output
    \item Conversion attempts to \texttt{variable} and \texttt{shared variable} types proved unsuccessful
\end{itemize}

The final solution implements:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A temporary variable \texttt{temp\_mem} within a wrapper procedure \texttt{load\_program\_memory}
    \item Proper signal driving from the temporary variable to the top-level \texttt{program\_memory}
\end{itemize}

% add procedure
\begin{lstlisting}[language=VHDL,caption={Load program memory procedure}]
  -- Procedure to load memory (avoiding shared variable issues)
    procedure load_program_memory(
        file_name : in string;
        signal mem : out program_array) is
        variable temp_mem : program_array := (others => (others => '0'));
    begin
        read_ihex_file(file_name, temp_mem);
        mem <= temp_mem;
    end procedure;
\end{lstlisting}


\subsection{Testbench Control Flow}
The stimulus process executes the following sequence:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Calls \texttt{load\_program\_memory} to initialize instructions
    \item Deactivates the reset signal
    \item Waits for 100 clock periods
    \item Asserts \texttt{simulation\_done} to force the clock signal to 0
\end{itemize}

% add process
\begin{lstlisting}[language=VHDL,caption={Stimulus process loading program memory}]
 stimulus: process
        constant hex_file_path : string := "/home/mdaoudi/Documents/ELEC-E9540/PIC16F84A/piklab/testbench3.hex";
    begin
        -- Load HEX file into program memory
        load_program_memory(hex_file_path, program_memory);
        
        -- Release reset after 2 clock cycles
        wait for CLK_PERIOD * 2.5;
        reset <= '0';
        
        -- Let the simulation run for a while
        wait for CLK_PERIOD * 100;
        
        -- End simulation
        simulation_done <= true;
        wait;
    end process;
\end{lstlisting}


\subsection{Instruction Fetch Implementation}
The instruction fetch mechanism operates by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Converting the program counter value \texttt{pc} to integer \texttt{pc\_int} on each falling edge
    \item Using \texttt{pc\_int} to index the \texttt{program\_memory} array
\end{itemize}

%add process
\begin{lstlisting}[language=VHDL,caption={Instruction Fetch Process}]
  instruction_fetch: process(clk)
        variable pc_int : integer;
    begin
        if falling_edge(clk) and reset = '0' then
            -- Convert PC to integer for array indexing
            pc_int := to_integer(unsigned(pc));
            
            -- Fetch instruction from program memory
            if pc_int < inst_mem_size then
                instruction <= program_memory(pc_int);
            else
                -- Handle PC out of bounds (optional)
                instruction <= (others => '0');
                report "PC out of bounds: " & integer'image(pc_int) severity warning;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

\end{lstlisting}

